course: dictionary
lesson: 11321
title: Words that travel: Yiddish
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words travel from one language to another, and English has borrowed many words that started out in other languages. Yiddish is a language, and it is also a clue to where a word has been. When you meet a word you do not know, ask: does it come from another language?
explain: Yiddish is spoken by many Jewish people, especially those whose families came from Central and Eastern Europe. It grew from German, with words from Hebrew and Slavic languages, and it is written in Hebrew letters. So a Yiddish word can look very different from English on the page.
explain: «"Borscht", the beetroot soup closely linked to Ukraine, reached English through Yiddish from the Slavic word "борщ".» The clue is the word through. Borscht did not jump straight into English: it passed through Yiddish on the way. Words often have a journey like this.
explain: English has borrowed many Yiddish words, such as glitch, schmooze, klutz, chutzpah and bagel. You may not know what these words mean yet, but the sentences around them can give you clues. If you are unsure, check a dictionary.
fact: Yiddish is a noun for a language, and the adjective Yiddish describes things linked to that language, such as Yiddish words.
fact: Yiddish is spoken by many Jewish people, especially those whose families came from Central and Eastern Europe.
fact: Yiddish grew from German, with words from Hebrew and Slavic languages.
fact: Yiddish is written in Hebrew letters.
fact: English has borrowed many words from Yiddish, such as «glitch», «schmooze», «klutz», «chutzpah» and «bagel».
exercise: «"Borscht", the beetroot soup closely linked to Ukraine, reached English through Yiddish from the Slavic word "борщ".» Through which language did borscht reach English? | Greek | *Yiddish | Latin | French
exercise: Yiddish is written in which letters? | *Hebrew letters | Roman letters, like the ones in this sentence | Chinese characters | Greek letters
exercise: Which of these words has been borrowed into English from Yiddish? | Table | Garden | *Bagel | Window
test: Yiddish grew from which language, with words from Hebrew and Slavic languages? | Arabic | Spanish | *German | French
test: Where did the families of many Yiddish speakers come from? | *Central and Eastern Europe | South America | Scandinavia | Southern Africa
exam: Which sentence about Yiddish is correct? | Yiddish is a language spoken by many Jewish people, and it is written in Hebrew letters | Yiddish is a city in Ukraine | *Yiddish is a language spoken by many Jewish people, and English has borrowed words from it | Yiddish is a type of beetroot soup
